---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DeepPhysicsPage from '@components/DeepPhysicsPage.astro';

const links = [
  { href: '/en/observables', label: 'Observables' },
  { href: '/en/tableau-periodique', label: 'Periodic table' },
  { href: '/en/relativite', label: 'Relativity' },
  { href: '/en/audit', label: 'Audit' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Nuclear Physics in PT" description="PT reading of the nuclear sector: cascade, magic numbers, binding energies, radioactivity, and validation.">
  <DeepPhysicsPage
    domain="physics"
    eyebrow="Nuclear physics"
    title="The nucleus as a depth of persistence"
    dek="PT treats the nuclear domain as an observation layer of the same structure: the channels that give Standard Model constants recombine into nuclear force, closures, and binding energies."
    heroNote="colour → pions → NN potential → shells → radioactivity"
    plainLabel="Plain language"
    standardLabel="Standard"
    technicalLabel="Technical"
    statusLabel="Status"
    stats={[
      { label: 'magic numbers', value: '2,8,20…126', note: 'nuclear closures reconstructed' },
      { label: 'reported validation', value: '408/413', note: 'passing tests, zero fitted parameter' },
      { label: 'status', value: 'DER/VAL', note: 'derived cascade, then compared with data' },
    ]}
    guide={{
      title: 'The nucleus is a deeper chemistry',
      paragraphs: [
        'One can enter nuclear physics without starting from equations. An atom has two layers of organization: outside, electrons make ordinary chemistry; at the centre, protons and neutrons make a much more compact chemistry.',
        'In chemistry, electrons fill shells. When a shell is closed, the atom becomes especially stable: noble gases are the familiar example. In the nucleus, there is an analogous idea, but with protons and neutrons, and with the strong force as the dominant channel.',
        'Magic numbers are the sizes at which a nuclear shell closes: 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82, 126. A nucleus near such a closure resists deformation better, binds more cleanly, and may change how it decays.',
        'PT therefore does not treat the nucleus as a separate continent. It asks what the same persistence logic becomes when degrees of freedom are trapped in a tiny, dense volume dominated by colour and pions.',
        'The idea to keep: nuclear stability is not just “a lot of strong force”. It is an architecture of channels. Some positions close a shell; others leave a fragile boundary.',
      ],
      cards: [
        { title: 'Shells', body: 'Protons and neutrons occupy levels; some closures make the nucleus more stable.' },
        { title: 'Spin-orbit', body: 'In the nucleus, the strong channel greatly amplifies spin-orbit splitting.' },
        { title: 'Radioactivity', body: 'A closure can suppress alpha preformation and change lifetimes.' },
      ],
    }}
    standard={{
      title: 'How PT computes the nuclear sector',
      paragraphs: [
        'The monograph organizes the sector into eight families: equations, nucleon-nucleon potential, binding energies, magic numbers, radioactivity, reactions, NLO phases, and ab initio calculations.',
        'The computation starts from constants already present in the theory: s=1/2, Nc=3, CF=4/3, and the effective strong coupling. From there, PT reconstructs a nuclear potential, then binding terms.',
        'The binding terms resemble a Bethe-Weizsäcker-type mass formula: volume, surface, Coulomb, asymmetry, pairing, closure corrections. The difference is that each term is read through the PT dictionary.',
        'The important point is that these terms are not knobs adjusted to rescue a curve. They are inherited from the channel cascade and then confronted with data.',
      ],
      flow: ['s=1/2', 'Nc / CF', 'αs', 'VNN', 'shells'],
    }}
    visual={{
      kind: 'nuclear',
      title: 'Why magic numbers appear',
      paragraphs: [
        'Without spin-orbit, the shell structure recovers the first closures 2, 8, and 20. It remains too poor, however, to force the large nuclear closures.',
        'With PT spin-orbit, the strong channel amplifies splitting: orbitals 0f7/2, 0g9/2, 0h11/2, and 0i13/2 drop and open the large gaps 28, 50, 82, and 126.',
        'The diagram is a structural reading of these closures. It does not replace the spectrum calculation; it shows where the spin-orbit bifurcation becomes decisive.',
      ],
      cards: [
        { title: '2, 8, 20', body: 'Closures already visible in shell structure without strong LS.' },
        { title: '28, 50, 82, 126', body: 'Large closures opened by nuclear spin-orbit amplification.' },
        { title: 'Validation', body: 'The nuclear sector is DER/VAL, not a pure arithmetic identity.' },
      ],
    }}
    technical={{
      title: 'Technical demonstration: cascade, magic numbers, limits',
      paragraphs: [
        'The nuclear chapter belongs to physical validation: outputs are compared with experiment and structural tests. Its status is not that of a pure arithmetic identity such as GFT or T5, but of a derived cascade whose consequences are confronted with data.',
        'The technical cascade is s=1/2 → Nc → CF → αs → σQCD → fπ,mπ,MN → VNN → Ebind. Each stage inherits constants derived in earlier chapters.',
        'The 408/413 score mixes algebraic, structural, and empirical tests; it should be read as a large coherence test, not as 413 independent constants.',
      ],
      steps: [
        { code: '1', title: 'constants', body: 's, μ*, Nc, CF, αEM, and αs come from previous sectors.' },
        { code: '2', title: 'colour', body: 'The colour channel fixes the strong residual between nucleons.' },
        { code: '3', title: 'pions', body: 'fπ, mπ, and MN feed the nucleon-nucleon potential.' },
        { code: '4', title: 'VNN', body: 'The potential combines pion, sigma, omega, and tensor components.' },
        { code: '5', title: 'binding', body: 'The mass formula receives volume, surface, Coulomb, asymmetry, and pairing terms.' },
        { code: '6', title: 'shells', body: 'D=2 supplies the four quantum numbers of the shell model.' },
        { code: '7', title: 'LS', body: 'PT spin-orbit opens closures 28, 50, 82, and 126.' },
        { code: '8', title: 'limits', body: 'Remaining failures concern monopole, three-body, and deformed-nucleus effects.' },
      ],
      cards: [
        { title: '408/413', body: 'Reported global coherence score for the nuclear sector.' },
        { title: 'about 132', body: 'Genuinely experimental comparisons according to the monograph reading.' },
        { title: 'Open', body: 'Deuteron, deformed nuclei, and EOS beyond 2n0 remain work areas.' },
      ],
    }}
    status={{
      title: 'Epistemic status',
      text: 'The nuclear sector is DER/VAL: the cascade is derived from PT constants and then compared with data. It is strong as a coherence test, but should not be presented as a pure theorem.',
      linksTitle: 'Related pages',
      links,
    }}
  />
</BaseLayout>
